HYBRID-006 — Scenario Semantics Review

Type: HYBRID (Agent + Human)
Sprint: Sprint 2
Owner: Agent (implementation complete), Alex (UX governor review)
Depends on:

AGENTIC-005 — Scenario Persistence (S2-002)

HUMAN-002 — Sprint 2 UX & Semantic Lock

🎯 Intent

Validate that persisted scenarios in the authenticated experience:

Preserve their pre-deal, exploratory meaning

Do not imply commitment, submission, or deal readiness

Align with the Sprint 2 UX Intent Doc

This ticket exists to prevent semantic drift, not to add features.

🧭 Review Scope (Strictly Limited)

This review covers only:

How scenarios are described in copy, labels, and headings

How history is framed (language + ordering)

What users are implicitly told a scenario is

This ticket does not:

Add new functionality

Change persistence logic

Introduce editing UI

Introduce deal or property concepts

🔍 UX Semantics Review Checklist (Governor)

Review the authenticated scenario experience against the following:

1. Scenario Meaning

Confirm that a scenario is framed as:

 Exploratory

 Non-binding

 Editable / revisitable

 Safe to create and revisit

Reject if a scenario feels like:

an offer

a submission

a finalized artifact

something the user “sent”

2. Language Audit (Hard Rules)

The following must NOT appear anywhere in the scenario experience:

“Submit”

“Apply”

“Finalize”

“Send”

“Approve”

“Offer”

“Lock in”

“Deal”

Preferred framing examples (exact wording flexible):

“What you explored”

“A scenario you created”

“An earlier version”

“You can revisit or reshape this”

3. History Framing

Confirm that history:

 Is chronological and narrative

 Feels informal and exploratory

 Does not imply legal or contractual record-keeping

Reject if history feels like:

version control

audit logs

deal revision tracking

4. Property Association Semantics

Confirm that:

 Scenarios do not require a property

 Property-less scenarios are framed as valid

 No copy implies a scenario must become property-bound

Acceptable framing:

“This scenario isn’t tied to a specific property yet.”

5. Deal Boundary Integrity

Confirm that no deal concepts exist, explicitly or implicitly:

 No Deal IDs

 No status states (draft / pending / approved)

 No multi-party language

 No next-step CTAs that imply transaction

If any of these appear → reject.

✅ Acceptance Criteria

This ticket is complete when one of the following outcomes is recorded:

✅ Approved as-is

✏️ Approved with minor copy or framing changes (list exact changes)

❌ Rejected due to semantic drift (explain violation)

No screenshots or logs required unless helpful.

⚠️ Common Failure Modes (Auto-Reject)

Scenarios feel “saved” in a way that implies seriousness

History feels like a legal or deal record

UX implies progression toward a transaction

Language suggests obligation or expectation

🧠 Governor’s Note

This ticket is the last semantic guardrail before Sprint 2 closes.

If scenarios feel heavier than exploration,
the sprint has failed its intent — even if persistence works perfectly.
